From: dewinter at candiensten.nl (Willem de Winter)
Date: Mon, 30 Apr 2001 22:15:06 +0200
Subject: SUMMARY: Odds Ratio and Relative Risk
Message-ID: <3AEDC7CA.A918B30E@candiensten.nl>

Dear all,

Many thanks to Brad Biggerstaff, Bill Pikounis, Don MacQueen, John
Sorkin, James Handfield, Andreas Christman, Chris Barker and Stefano
Calza for their help in answering my rather naive question (not
surprisingly, it's not my own field)on Odds Ratios and Relative Risks.
Nevertheless, I received several requests for answers, so here's a
summary. The short answer appears that Odds Ratios can be computed by
logistic regression and Relative Risks by Cox Proportional Hazards
regression. Brad Biggerstaff provided a useful function for an RR.

The original question was:

I was asked if S-PLUS has functions to calculate odds ratios and
relative risks (often used in biostatistics). I couldn't find anything
ready-made, and although it isn't difficult to quickly cobble together a
simple function for e.g. an OR, I was wondering if someone has already
developed (a library of) functions for OR, RR and any associated
(biostatistical) procedures. Or did I overlook them?

---------------------------------------------------------------------
John Sorkin:
Odds ratios are computed by logistic regression
Relative risks are computed by Cox Proportional Hazards regression.

You can perform these analyses using glm() and coxph() respectively. You
might want to read the Guide to Statistics, vol 1 and vol 2 that come
with
S-Plus 
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Don Macqueen:
You could use the glm() function to fit logistic regression models 
with dichotomous predictors. Then exp(parameter estimate) is an odds 
ratio. That would be one way.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bill Pikounis:
Frank Harrell's Design and Hmisc libraries comprehensively handle the
above
subject matter and many "associated (biostatistical) procedures." These
come
bundled with SPLUS 2000, and if you are using something else, check out
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/splus.html and other pages in
the
vicinity.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
James Handsfield:
check out the hmisc and design libraries written by Frank Harrell.
You can get documentation for these at Frank's web site:
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/index.html.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Andreas Christmann:
Often, odds ratios are calculated after modelling a logistic regression.
Logistic regression is a special case of generalized linear models.
In S-Plus you can use the function glm with family=binomial .
If you use the canonical link function , i.e.
family=binomial(link=logit),
the estimated parameters are log odds ratios.

With ?glm you can find information on this function.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Chris Barker:
Check Frank Harrell's hmisc library for the lrm function (in the context
of
logistic regression). There may well be other procedures for OR and RR
there
as well

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stefano:
Here are two possible solutions (of course they do the same thing):

OR.sum <- function(glm.obj,coverage=.95,two.sided=T)
{
         s <- summary(glm.obj)
         z <- ifelse(two.sided==F,qnorm(coverage), qnorm((1 +
coverage)/2))
         margin <- s$coefficients[,2]*z
         glm.lower <- glm.obj$coefficients-margin
         glm.upper <- glm.obj$coefficients+margin
         OR <- exp(glm.obj$coefficients)
         lower <- exp(glm.lower)
         upper <- exp(glm.upper)
         cbind(s$coeff,OR,lower,upper)
}

## Kindly suggested by Bendix Carstensen

OR.ci <-
function( obj, alpha=0.05 ) {
if ( !(obj$family$family == "binomial" & obj$family$link == "logit" ) )
{
  stop( paste ( "\nThis function is only meaningful for binomial
family",
                "with logit link\nnot for",
                obj$family$family, "family with", obj$family$link,
"link" ) )
}
ci <- exp( summary(obj)$coef[,1:2]  %*%
            rbind( c( 1,  1, 1 ),
                   c( 0, -1, 1 ) * qnorm( 1-alpha/2 ) ) )
colnames( ci ) <- c( "OR", "low", "hi" )
ci
}

Maybe you'd better have a look at Mark Myatt's intorductory notes 
(http://www.myatt.demon.co.uk)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Brad Biggerstaff:

You can get odds ratios from the S-Plus function glm by exponentiating
the
coefficients of a fit with family=binomial and link=logit.  (In S-Plus
2000,
at least, these are automatically set when fitting a logistic regression
using the menu: Statistics > Regression > Logistic... )

You might also look in Frank Harrell's Design library, the function lrm
(for
logistic regression model).  http://lib.stat.cmu.edu/DOS/S/Harrell/

As for the relative risk: if you're able to estimate this directly
(e.g.,
from a cohort study, not a case-control study) then this is really just
the
ratio of two independent binomial probabilities (again, depending on the
design...it's conditional on total sample sizes).  Point estimation is
straightforward enough (when the denominator probability is not
estimated
with zero), but interval estimation is trickier.  I include code below
to go
about this; it's based on the paper Comparative analysis of two rates,
Miettinen & Nurminen,
_Statistics in Medicine_, 4:213-226, 1985.

Cheers,

brad

Brad Biggerstaff, Ph.D.
Mathematical Statistician
Division of Vector-Borne Infectious Diseases
CDC/National Center for Infectious Diseases
PO Box 2087 Fort Collins, CO  80522-2087
970.221.6473 +++ bbiggerstaff at cdc.gov

# Function to compute confidence intervals for the ratio
# of two independent binomial probabilities
#
"binom.rr.ci"<-
function(x1, n1, x2, n2, alpha = 0.05, plot.it = F)
{
# binom.rr.ci
#  
# Produces a 100(1-alpha)% confidence interval for the ratio 
# of two independent binomial probabilities, using the CI from
# Miettinen & Nurminen, Comparative analysis of two rates, 
# _Statistics in Medicine_, 4:213-226, 1985
#
# Required arguments:
#
# x1,x2: number of 'successes' for binomal variates
# n1,n2: number of observations for each 
#
# Optional arguments:
#
# alpha: confidence coefficient
#
# Author:
#
# Brad Biggerstaff
# Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
# National Center for Infectious Diseases
# Division of Vector-Borne Infectious Diseases
# P.O. Box 2087  Fort Collins, CO  80522-2087
# (970) 221-6473      ...        bkb5 at cdc.gov
#
        chi2RR <- function(rr, x1, n1, x2, n2, alpha)
        {
                n <- n1 + n2
                A <- n * rr
                B <-  - (n1 * rr + x1 + n2 + x2 * rr)
                C <- x1 + x2
                R2 <- ( - B - sqrt(B^2 - 4 * A * C))/(2 * A)
                R1 <- R2 * rr
                V <- (((R1 * (1 - R1))/n1 + ((rr^2) * R2 * (1 - R2))/n2) *
n)/(
                        n - 1)
                r1 <- x1/n1
                r2 <- x2/n2
                X2 <- ((r1 - r2 * rr)^2)/V
                chisq.crit <- qchisq(1 - alpha, 1)
                X2 - chisq.crit
        }
        point.est <- (x1/n1)/(x2/n2)
        lower.limit <- uniroot(chi2RR, c(1e-007, point.est), x1 = x1, n1 =
n1, 
                x2 = x2, n2 = n2, alpha = alpha)$root
        upper.limit <- uniroot(chi2RR, c(point.est, 1000), x1 = x1, n1 = n1,
x2
                 = x2, n2 = n2, alpha = alpha)$root
        ans <- data.frame(point.est = point.est, lower.limit = lower.limit, 
                upper.limit = upper.limit, confidence = 1 - alpha)
        if(plot.it) {
                par(xaxs = "s", yaxs = "s")
                RR <- seq(max(0, 0.8 * ans$lower.limit), 1.2 *
ans$upper.limit, 
                        len = 250)
                chisq.crit <- qchisq(1 - alpha, 1)
                plot(RR, chi2RR(RR, x1, n1, x2, n2, alpha = alpha) +
chisq.crit,
                        type = "l", xlab = "RR", ylab = "Chi-squared
Statistic"
                        )
                abline(h = chisq.crit, lty = 2)
                title(paste("Chi-squared statistic for risk ratio of two
independent binomials\nand associated ",
                        round(100 * (1 - alpha), 2), "% confidence
interval"), 
                        sep = "", cex = 0.75)
                segments(ans$lower.limit, 0, ans$lower.limit, chisq.crit,
lty
                         = 2)
                segments(ans$upper.limit, 0, ans$upper.limit, chisq.crit,
lty
                         = 2)
        }
        ans
}

From a296180 at mica.fmr.com  Tue May  1 12:47:12 2001
From: a296180 at mica.fmr.com (David Kane <David Kane)
Date: Tue, 01 May 2001 08:47:12 -0400
Subject: Summary of Name mangling when using ifelse
Message-ID: <15086.43600.829354.583368@arbres2a>

Thanks to Bill Dunlap, Jingshan Zhang, Don MacQueen, Tim Hesterberg, Tamara
Shatar, David Brahm, Nick Ellis, Rolf Turner, and Alan Zaslavsky for their 
advice on
this. The short answer is that my problem had nothing to do with ifelse;
instead, I was subsetting out a matrix, rather than a vector, with df["x"]. I
should have used df[["x"]]. Here are some of the gory details (mostly already
provided by Alan Zaslavsky):

Recall the initial set-up:

> version
Version 6.0 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.6 : 2000 
> df <- data.frame(x = 1:3)
> df$new <- ifelse(df["x"] > 2, 1, 0)
> df
  x new.x 
1 1     0
2 2     0
3 3     1

The problem, as several people pointed out, is that df["x"] is a dataframe,
which ifelse, reasonably enough, converts to a matrix.

> class(df["x"])
[1] "data.frame"
> class(ifelse(df["x"] > 2, 1, 0))
[1] "matrix"

By assigning this matrix to an element in df, I go beyond my S+ knowledge. I
now have a dataframe with two elements: x (a vector) and new (a matrix).

> is.vector(df$x)
[1] T
> is.matrix(df$new)
[1] T

Nick Ellis noted that "When you append a matrix "new" to a data frame, the
column names are of the form new.x, new.y, where x and y are the column names
of the matrix." Bill Dunlap explains that this is what causes the printing
behavior that I see. He writes:

#################
The function format.data.frame() is called by print() to format the data and it
sees that df$new is a matrix (with 1 column named "x") so it pastes together
"new" and "x".

One way out of this is to change format.data.frame, adding in front of the line

                        if(length(cn <- dimnames(xi)[[2]]))

the lines

                        if(dim(xi)[2] == 1)
                                cn <- names(x)[i]
                        else

so it looks like

                        if(dim(xi)[2] == 1)
                                cn <- names(x)[i]
                        else if(length(cn <- dimnames(xi)[[2]]))
#################

My problem, of course, is that I shouldn't have used df["x"]. I should have
used df[["x"]]. Several people also suggested df[,"x"]. Note that these
constructs seem to be identical.

> identical(df[,"x"], df[["x"]])
[1] T

Rolf Turner noted that:

#################

(1) The construction you describe works O.K. for me in Splus 3.4
under Solaris 2.7.  So this looks like another bug introduced while
``improving'' the package.

(2) ``df'' is not a good name to use for a data frame, since it is
the name of a builtin function (the density function of F
distributions); but I doubt that this is the source of your problem.

#################

Thanks to all concerned for spending time on my problem.

Dave Kane

