Thanks for the summary Willem.  I have one comment about risk
ratios and the Cox model.  The Cox model produces a hazard
ratio, which many people refer to as a risk ratio.  As far
as I know, risk has two meanings.  In economics it refers
roughtly to the probability of a loss times the amount of the loss
if it occurs.  In epidemiology and biostatistics risk is
often taken to mean probability.  A risk is not a rate,
so it is not quite right to refer to a hazard ratio (which
is a rate ratio) as a risk ratio. -Frank Harrell

