On Thu, 3 May 2001, Tord Snall wrote:

> try stepAIC() in MASS. There is also something in leaps but I havn't tried it.

leaps() is the better answer here (as I wrote privately).  stepAIC, step,
lm, add1 or drop1 all are designed to work correctly with factor
`independent vars' and hierarchies of terms, which leaps ignores.  So only
use leaps if you just have unrelated continuous predictors.  It's a good
tool for that job (although the limit on the number of vars is from another
computing age), but the job is quite unusual.  There is also the question
of whether variable selection (rather than something like ridge regression)
is a good idea once there are more than a few continuous predictors,
and I think it rarely is.

