> IF I use Splus 4.5 or 5.0 , I get
> >Intercept  x
>       1.05 -1
> (These two fit are very different!)
>
>     Does anyone knows the LMS algorithms that are implemented in these
> different versions of Splus? If they are both approximation algorithms,
> which one is more preferable or more stable?  Could anyone give me some
> references about the related or exact algorithms to calulate the least
> median squares (LMS)?

I gave up on lmsreg when this happened, and when ltsreg got changed to use
90% trimming (and so be non-resistant).  One issue is what the `median'
means.  That has changed between the versions, but not documented. Try

> lmsreg(x,y, quan=3)$coef
 Intercept x
       0.3 1
(S-PLUS 6.0)

In all (?) versions it is documented to be floor(n/2) + floor((p+1)/2),
but the code is now floor((n + p + 1)/2), which is not the same in this
example.  There are warnings about this in V&R 3rd ed.

I prefer lqs in library MASS, which is *much* faster, and does actually
work as documented.

The best reference to reliable code is

   A. Marazzi (1993) Algorithms, Routines and S Functions for Robust
   Statistics. Wadsworth & Brooks/Cole.

For later developments (as used in lqs) see

   P. Rousseeuw and M. Hubert (1997) Recent developments in PROGRESS. In
   L1-Statistical Procedures and Related Topics ed Y. Dodge, IMS Lecture
   Notes volume 31, pp. 201-214.

