From hatfield at solander.bc.ca  Thu May  3 18:57:33 2001
From: hatfield at solander.bc.ca (Todd Hatfield)
Date: Thu, 03 May 2001 11:57:33 -0700
Subject: Summary: overlaying time series data in a plot
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.0.20010503082422.01d34950@solander.bc.ca>

Sincere thanks to: Anne York, James Holtman, Renaud Lancelot, Deb
Carlson, and Patrick Connolly for helping me with my question regarding
overlaying time series data in a plot.

There were a number of possible solutions, but most were variations on a
theme.  The most straightforward solution uses xyplot, since Trellis
is explicitly designed to handle levels.  One can choose to have the
plots separate or superposed using panel=panel.superpose in the xyplot
function call.  

All solutions required that I create additional variables
"year", and "day of the year" from my dates. 
The year variable is easily computed with the function years().  The
following function, which was assembled from Renaud Lancelot's reply,
will compute day of the year:

day.of.year <- function(x) {1 + floor(365.25 * (as.numeric(x)/365.25 -
floor(as.numeric(x)/365.25)))}

An equivalent function "yeardays()" exists in Splus 6.0. 
(Perhaps there's something similar in Splus 2000, but I didn't find
it.)

After creating year and dayofyear, the following did what I was looking
for:

xyplot(reservoir.level ~ dayofyear, data="" groups=year,
type="l", panel=panel.superpose) ## superimposed data

xyplot(reservoir.level ~ dayofyear | year, data="" type="l")
##  separate plots

# add the following to get nice x-axis labels

# scales = list(x = list(at = seq(1, 366, 2 * 30.44) + 30.44/2, labels =
month.abb[c(1,3,5,7,9,11)]))

# add panel functions to get grids, loess lines, reference lines,
etc.

Alternative:

lapply(split(seq(data$level),years(data$date)),function(x){ 

lines(yeardays(data$date[x]),data$level[x]) 

})

You would have to initialize the plot area for 1:366 on the
x-axis.

Original posting:

I have a dataframe with two columns: a column with dates (in daily steps
for 15 years), and a column with corresponding reservoir levels. Rather
than plotting the data as a continuous time series, I'd like to create a
plot that spans one year in length, with data for each year (as a line)
overlaid. I'd also like to add a line indicating mean, or percentile. I'm
wondering if anyone has a function that does something like this. If not,
any tips would be most welcome for how to manipulate the data to
facilitate construction of such a plot. Dealing with leap years is one of
the problems I'm having.

Thank you.

(S+ 2k R3, Win98)

