From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri May  4 19:13:42 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Fri, 04 May 2001 20:13:42 +0100
Subject: Re: Maximum Memory Size in S-PLUS 2000
In-Reply-To: <3AF2F4CE.A590E47E@math.mun.ca>
References: <3AF2F4CE.A590E47E@math.mun.ca>
Message-ID: <Pine.WNT.4.31.0105042008420.138-100000@tern.stats>

On Fri, 4 May 2001, Paul Y. Peng wrote:

> Dear S-PLUS users or developers,
>
> I know that S-PLUS 2000 comes with a default value of 2147483647
> for options()$memory. My question is: Can I increase this default
> value to, say, 10GB (Suppose that I have enough free HD space to
> do so)?

Not unless you have a 64-bit Intel chip (and not even then).  Windows
allocates up to 2GB (that number + 1) for user address space, except on
some versions of Windows Server which allow 3Gb (so the docs say, and this
is Microsoft).

> Recently I am working with a large data set of about 75MB using
> coxph(). No matter how large this value I set above 2147483647,

It is computed as a 32-bit signed integer, I believe.

> coxph stops whenever S-PLUS uses memory near 2147483647, with an
> error message saying "... Unable to obtain requested dynamic
> memory ..." (I use Taskinfo to watch the memory usage, and by
> the time coxph stops, it shows that I still have 2GB free virtual
> memory within the maximum 4GB swap space.) It seems that you can
> only lower the default value, and raising it up does not have any
> effect. I hope that is not correct. Any hints or help are highly
> appreciated.

It is correct.  AFAIK only certain Unix versions of S-PLUS can use more
than 2Gb.  That said, 2Gb looks enough for 75Mb objects, so I suggest
you find out why you are needing so much memory.

