On Mon, 7 May 2001 jdiggans at genelogic.com wrote:

>
> We're running the Solaris version of SPlus (v 6.0)  and it's capable of
> using enormous amounts of swap space. We have 2 GB of RAM on this box and
> have allocated another 11GB of swap. We've seen some larger models use up
> to 4 GB worth of swap after burning through the available RAM. If you're
> looking to do large-scale modeling the limitations of the MS Windows
> platform render it nearly unuseable.

Um:
toucan% file Sqpe
Sqpe:           ELF 32-bit MSB executable SPARC Version 1, dynamically
linked, not stripped

shows that S-PLUS 6.0 on Solaris (at least ours) is a 32-bit application.
So I don't see how it could address more than 4Gb.  (64-bit applications
can under Solaris, of course, and that includes the OS kernel.)

ix86 Linux on the other hand is a 32-bit OS, again I believe with a
4Gb address limit.  The difference between that and Windows' limits
are small I believe: the limits are often of the software rather than the
platform.

R vs S-PLUS is another question.  R can run as 64-bit on Solaris, but not
on ix86 Linux.  Whether R needs less or more space depends on what the
problem is.

> A following-up question: Does Linux (Redhat7.x on intel chips)
> have the same limitation? I am thinking about to try the fit
> on a Linux machine with R. Thanks again.

