From ezivot at u.washington.edu  Fri May 11 22:21:29 2001
From: ezivot at u.washington.edu (Eric Zivot)
Date: Fri, 11 May 2001 15:21:29 -0700
Subject: Re: Non-S question; Dicussion point: least squares vs. ML
In-Reply-To: <5.0.2.1.0.20010511145249.02bf36d0@uidaho.edu>
References: <5.0.2.1.0.20010511145249.02bf36d0@uidaho.edu>
Message-ID: <GPECKMJDPHIDALEELIMHEENLCBAA.ezivot@u.washington.edu>

A good source is Davidson and MacKinnon, Estimation and Inference in
Econometrics, Oxford University Press, chapter 7 (Instrumental Variables).
They give a nice summary of finite sample results for IV and OLS estimation
in structural equation modeling. The problem with OLS, of course, is that it
is not consistent in general in a structural equation because of the
correlation between the endogenous variables and the regresson error terms.
The simultaneous equations estimators (IV, 2SLS, GMM, LIML etc) give
consistent estimators but may suffer from higher variance than OLS. This has
led some to favor OLS even though it is not consistent. I find this logic
hard to understand. Certain ML estimators (limited information for example)
suffer from the the nonexistence of moments which can lead to crazy
estimates. In general, the quality of the ML procedures depends on the
quality of the "instruments" used to achieve consistency. If the instruments
are of bad quality (e.g not correlated with the endogenous variables) then
the resulting estimates will be quite bad. Some recent references are

0. Bound, Jeager and Baker, "Problems with instrumental variables estimation
when the correlation between the instruments and the endogenous explanatory
variables is weak," JASA, 90, 1995.
1. Buse, "The bias of instrumental variables estimators," Econometrica, 60,
1992
2. Hall, Rudebusch and Wilcox, "Judging instrument relevance in instrumental
variales estimation," International Economic Review, 1996
3. Staiger and Stock, "Instrumental variables regression with weak
instruments," Econometrica, 65, 1997
4. Zivot, Startz and Nelson, "valid confidence intervals and inference in
the presence of weak instruments," International Economic Review, 1998

Hope this helps, ez

