Consider the following behaviour:

> # Version 5.1:
> x <- c(a=1,b=2,c=3)
S+5.1 > x
 a b c 
 1 2 3
S+5.1 > dput(x,"x")
S+5.1 > y <- dget("x")
S+5.1 > y
$a:
[1] 1

$b:
[1] 2

$c:
[1] 3

> # Not what was wanted/expected.  I.e. we stored away a 3-vector
> # and got back a list with 3 scalar components.
> #
> # In 3.1 things are O.K.:

> x <- c(a=1,b=2,c=3)
S+3.4 > x
 a b c 
 1 2 3
S+3.4 > dput(x,"x")
S+3.4 > y <- dget("x")
S+3.4 > y
 a b c 
 1 2 3

> # That of course ***is*** what I wanted/expected.

Is this a bug or a ``feature'' in 5.1?  If it's a ``feature''
what the hell good is it?  If it's a bug, is it a known one?
I don't ***recall*** seeing any discussion on this issue.  (But
that could just be Old-Timers' Disease.)  Does this have something
to do with the new system of ``classes'' in S 4?

The ``bug'' is easy enuff to work around in any particular instance
in which one is aware of it, but seems to me to be a lurking danger.

