"John Sorkin" <john at grecc.ab.umd.edu> writes:

> I have recently begun to use LME to analyze longitudinal data. In the past,
> I have used SAS PROC MIXED to analyze the data. In order to determine if I
> am using LME properly, I have compared the results of S-PLUS LME to SAS PROC
> MIXED. As you will see below, the results are not the same.
> Could someone tell me what mistake I have made in my call to LME? I have
> included both a SHORT SUMMARY of the results of the two analyses and a
> COMPLETE LISTING of the results of the two analyses below. I am running
> S-Plus 2000 and SAS 8.0 on a Windows NT platform.

As far as I can see you are fitting different models.  Notice that the
SAS PROC MIXED model has 6 covariance parameters (a 3 by 3 symmetric
matrix) but the lme model has 3 (a 2 by 2 symmetric matrix).

I know what the lme model is but I don't know what the SAS model is.

When I am cross-checking lme results against PROC MIXED I use the
"random" statement for PROC MIXED, not the "repeated" statement.  (I
have difficulty remembering what the model generated by the "repeated"
statement is.)  I think the "random" statement corresponding to the lme
model would be

   random time / type=un subject=child_id;

Another thing to check is whether the observations in your SAS data
set are sorted by child_id.  SAS PROC MIXED requires that you have the
observations sorted in order of the grouping factor.  This is not
required in lme.

> SHORT SUMMARY:
> 
> 
