> > 
> > Exact limits on the ratio or difference of two independent binomial
> > proportions, Nathan Mantel, Biometrics, ?:623, 1988.  
> (sorry about the
> > sketchy info there...it's a letter to the editor)

and a discussion with refence to Bishop, Fienberg, and Holland (1975) was
provided by Ray Falk:
> Hello Bob Baskin,
> 
> For your 4th sample, you definitely want to return to a 
> multinomial model
> and compare margin 4 with margin 1 from the two-way (3*3) table (using
> levels of Yes, No, and Missing at each of the repeated 
> measures and testing
> Yes4/(Yes4+No4)-Yes1/(Yes1+No1)=0).  You will need to compute the
> (9-element) multinomial covariance matrix along with the 
> appropriate linear,
> logarithmic, and exponential functions and derivatives (as in Bishop,
> Fienberg, and Holland (1975)).  Multi-normality of the 
> multinomial sample
> proportions will support testing or confidence limit 
> estimation (provided
> the marginal distributions would support a chi-square approximation if
> independent (that is, provided the intermediate calculations 
> represent a
> sufficient amount of aggregation, in my opinion).  You might 
> contrive a
> continuity correction for use with this test to be 
> conservative.  Running
> the multinomial approximation through a combinatorially exact 
> calculation
> (StatXact?) would give you an exact rejection probability 
> (and, ultimately,
> exact confidence limits), but would implicitly assume permutational
> homogeneity under the null hypothesis (which may not be the case - the
> difference tends to be of comparable magnitude to that attributable to
> combinatorially exact inference)).
> 
> Regarding this last issue, the baseline response rate (margin 
> 1) may best
> represent the expected/hypothesized response subsequently 
> (margin 4), in
> which case the multinomial should be derived from a two-way 
> table with the
> margin 4 constrained to equal the fixed (observed)  baseline 
> value (margin
> 1).  Bishop, Fienberg, and Holland (1975) treat aspects of 
> this one-sample
> problem in detail.
> 
> I have treated the one-sample case, but assume you would be testing
> differences in changes over time between two classes of 
> respondents, in
> which case you have a two-sample difference in statistics of the form
> mentioned above.  In the two-sample problem, if one sample serves as a
> reference for the other, permutational homogeneity does not provide an
> accurate representation; however, application of the 
> reference multinomial
> covariance matrix for both samples provides a simple and 
> correct solution.
>

