Subject: John's mixed -> lme
Message-ID: <F7802H67yRLVbNDN4Cx00006db3@hotmail.com>

John Sorkin asked about fitting a longitudinal model in Splus similar
to one he had fitted in SAS.

I'm presuming this is the model you are interesting in getting in Splus.
>    proc mixed data=m.tbp order=internal;      class child_id;      model
> dxa_fat=time/solution;      repeated/type=un subject=child_id;    run;

>From your long listing it looks like there are 4 missing time observations. 
In some cases it doesn't matter but it's generally

better to explicitly let SAS know, even in those cases. Also,

because you are fitting a linear model in time, it involves a little

more work.

data temp; set m.tbp; cltime = time; run;
proc sort data = temp; by child_id time; run;

proc mixed data=temp order=internal;
 class     child_id  cltime;
 model     dxa_fat = time / solution;
 repeated  cltime  / type = un  subject = child_id;
run;

The 'cltime' is needed so SAS knows to match the "right" time with
the "right" observation. The variable on the repeated line needs
to be be a class variable so 'time' can't be used directly.

And this is the code that I think should get you the same results in
Splus.

child.fit1 <- lme(fixed       = DXA.FAT ~ TIME,
                 data        = child,
                 correlation = corSymm()      )
summary(child.fit1)

Also check that lme is fitting with REML. From memory it does this by
default although it may have changed from earlier versions.

(I'm not sure why you are using 'order=internal' which may mean I
have missed the point somewhere.)

Megan Pledger

