means of Akaike Information Criterion (AIC).  I tried to repeat the example
using S-plus (I was using the 'step' function from Prof. Ripley's book) but got

1) It's Venables & Ripley's book
2) The function is stepAIC, not step

very different AIC values from the book.  Much appreciated if anyone can help.

3) Log-likelhood and hence AIC is only defined up to an additive constant.
You can't compare models with discrete and models with continuous distributions
as the log-likelihoods are not comparable (different dominating measures,
and the models give mutually singular probability measures).  That's
why the Poisson value is so different.  If Lindsey's book really does as
you say, it is not correct.

4) stepAIC is designed to use AIC to compare models with different sets
of predictors, with no guarantee to be comparable across different families.
Given that most of the models you quote are actually not GLMs unless the
dispersion is known, and the log-likelihood depends on what you assume
about it, there is no way that the GLM specification can determine
the precise AIC.

>
