From timh at insightful.com  Wed May 16 17:02:30 2001
From: timh at insightful.com (Tim Hesterberg x319)
Date: Wed, 16 May 2001 10:02:30 -0700
Subject: Re: bootstrap and arg.stats
In-Reply-To: <00256A4E.00410518.00@westlb.co.uk>
References: <00256A4E.00410518.00@westlb.co.uk>
Message-ID: <200105161702.KAA10907@tomato.statsci.com>

Bevan Blair asked:
>Say I have a function my.func(data,arg). arg is just an arguement
>that I pass to the function. Say now I want to bootstrap this
>function using
>
>bootstrap(data, my.func, ........)
>
>The documentation states that you can use arg.stats in the bootstrap
>to pass the arg argument.
>
>My question is how do I actually do this? It says pass it as a list
>but there are no examples to work from.

Here are examples, from the draft help file for a future version
of bootstrap():

# Demonstrate the args.stat argument
#   without args.stat:
bootstrap(stack.loss, mean(stack.loss, trim=.2))
#   statistic is a function:
bootstrap(stack.loss, mean, args.stat = list(trim=.2))
#   statistic is an expression, object "h" defined in args.stat
bootstrap(stack.loss, mean(stack.loss, trim=h), 
          args.stat = list(h=.2))

FYI, we use args.stat rather than "..." because we
can't use ... for everything:
  - arguments to bootstrap methods (bootstrap will be generic)
  - arguments to the statistic
  - arguments to the sampler (e.g. sampling with unequal probabilities;
    future versions of bootstrap will use args.sampler)

