To S-News:

Point of clarification:

CENSORED data are data for which one knows only that the actual data value
are within some (possibly infinite) interval (right censored = (L,inf) ).

TRUNCATED data are data that are not present when they fall within some
(possibly infinite) interval.

For example, a particle counter that "sees" and counts only particles above
a minimal size will produce a truncated distribution. One will not know the
proportion of data falling below the minimum size without extra assumptions.

Different methodology is required to deal with truncated and censored data.
I believe that the data in this thread and most cases are censored, not
truncated.

