Ok, some correspondents had suggested outer() as the solution and here's
the most detailed suggestion. Thanks Baz!

---------- Forwarded message ----------
Date: Fri, 18 May 2001 17:43:56 +0100
From: B.Rowlingson at lancaster.ac.uk
To: Anna Legedza <anna at hsph.harvard.edu>
Subject: Re: [S] Summary: persp, perspp, pretty

Aargh!

 I meant to write to you before you got back to the list!

 I dont see why you are evaluating the density function over
a set of points, then interp()ing them, then using persp on
that interpolation. Better to call persp on the values themselves:

x <- seq(-10,10)
y <- seq(-10,10)

den2 <- outer(x, y, gdf, alpha = 1)
denout <- persp(x,y,den2)
lines(perspp(rep(margdose(0.5),length(x)),y,gdf(margdose(0.5),y),denout),col=7,lwd=2)

It can look at bit funny at odd angles:

denout_persp(x,y,den2,eye=c(2195,60,.2))

 but thats because Splus is drawing the surface using rectangles.
Increase the 
resolution and it looks much smoother!

 x_seq(-10,10,len=100)
 y_seq(-10,10,len=100) 
 den2_outer(x,y,gdf,alpha=.5)
 denout_persp(x,y,den2,eye=c(2195,60,2))

Postscript file here:
http://www.maths.lancs.ac.uk/~rowlings/Stats/gdf.ps

 hows that?

Baz

