From garrett at NRCan.gc.ca  Fri May 18 12:21:58 2001
From: garrett at NRCan.gc.ca (Garrett, Robert)
Date: Fri, 18 May 2001 08:21:58 -0400
Subject: Summary: Differences in Covariance Matrices
Message-ID: <3E3C279AF3F9D411BAA00002A529150E6CBAAD@S0-OTT-X10>

Last week I asked the following question: "When I use cov.wt with the vector
of weights from cov.mve I get identical estimates for the means and
correlations, but the estimates for the covariance matrices are different.
Why? I would have thought they should have been the same."

I received replies from Peter England, Krishna Padayachee and Jagadish
Rangrej - Thank you.

The crux of the matter lies within cov.mve as explained by Krishna
Padayachee:

        "The function cov.mve uses a resampling algorithm (Rousseeux and
Leroy - Robust methods and Outlier Detection, 1987 NY John Wiley)  to obtain
initial estimates of the mean T(X) and covariance matrix C(X) where X is the
data matrix.  A subsample J of the data is used in this calculation, J is
indicated by the vector mve.wt in an evaluation of cov.mve.

        The next step involves calculating the Mahalanobis distance for each
vector x in the subsample J and assigning a weight w(x)  to each x in J.  A
weighted mean T_{1}(X) and weighted cov. matrix C_{1}(X) is then calculated
based on the weights w.  It is this weighted mean T_{1}(X) and weighted cov.
matrix  C_{1}(X) that is reported by the function cov.mve

        Using the vector mve.wt  as a weight vector in cov.wt  will  NOT in
general give the weighted mean T_{1}(X) nor the weighted cov. matrix
C_{1}(X).  What you will get is T(X) as the "centre" and C(X) (modulo a
constant multiplicative factor)  as the estimated cov. matrix.

        Here are some references:
        1) Rouseeuw  and van Zomeren, JASA 1990 vol 85, No. 141. --- very
readable
        2) H. P. Lopuhuaa & P. Rouseeuw , Annals of Statistics 1991, V19,
No. 1

        As to which is preferable, cov.mve or cov.wt ,   I leave that up to
individuals with more computational and applied experience than myself."

Working with the data set confirms a recommendation from John Tukey that I
read many years ago to the effect, "Use a robust method, it doesn't matter
so much which one".  The differences in the results from different robust
approaches, and the geochemical interpretation of the results, are small in
comparison with the vast improvement of using robust estimation with "noisy
and contaminated" natural science data.

Thank you.

Robert G. (Bob) Garrett
