Subject: exact ci for a proportion: estimating rare events
Message-ID: <F3980C657FBFD1118DDD00A0C9AF98F5022F280B@brwsmxsusr01.brw.hmrag.com>

I am trying to provide guidance to our group in designing studies to
estimate the risk of rare events.  If we believe that a risk of a
disease event is rare (in the area of 1/10,000, 1/50,000, or 1/100,000),
but we have little reliable information on what the actual risk
parameter is in any specific population of interest, how can we design a
study to estimate this risk, or at least rule out certain alternative
levels of risk.

The S+ plot that I envision to help understand what studies of different
sample sizes can and cannot tell us is a plot of risk levels on the
Y-axis vs. various sample sizes on the X-axis. For instance, I would
posit that a risk is p=1/50,000, draw an abline for p=0.00002, and then
plot "exact" confidence intervals around that abline for the various N's
on the X-axis.  The approach in S+ would look something like this--but
this (and other approaches) does not work well:

> samp.sz
 [1]  1000  2500  5000  7500 10000 15000 20000 25000 30000 35000 40000
45000 50000

> f.ci
function(p = 1/100000, r = 1, n = samp.sz, Za = 1.96)
{
        ci.mat <- matrix(rep(0, (length(n) * 4)), nrow = length(n), ncol
= 4)
        dimnames(ci.mat) <- list(NULL, c("N", "lcl", "p", "ucl"))
        ci.mat[, 1] <- n
        ci.mat[, 3] <- rep(p, length(n))
        A <- 2 * r + (Za^2)
        q <- (1 - p)
        B <- Za * (sqrt(Za^2 + 4 * r * q))
        C <- 2 * (n + Za^2)
        ucl <- (A + B)/C
        lcl <- (A - B)/C
        ci.mat[, 2] <- lcl
        ci.mat[, 4] <- ucl
        ci.mat
}

> plot.ci
function(cl = ci.mat[, 4], uplim = max(ci.mat[, 4]))
{
#Illustration of precision (sampling error) of risk estimate
        plot(samp.sz, cl, type = "l", xlab = "Sample size (N)", axes =
F, ylab = "95% confidence limit for risk estimate", ylim = c(0, uplim), 
        main = "Risk=1/10000")
        box()
        axis(1, at = samp.sz)
        axis(2)
        abline(h = (1/100000))
        #abline(h = (1/50000))
        #abline(h = (1/10000))  
            lines(samp.sz, ci.mat[, 2])
        lines(samp.sz, ci.mat[, 4])
}

My difficulty is in finding or creating exact confidence intervals
specifying (fixing) the p and varying the N. The responses to Tim Wades
recent inquiry, [S] exact ci for a proportion [5/3/00], do not quite
serve my purpose.

Does anyone have any guidance to provide, either in terms of S+ code for
exact CI's, or about the sample size estimation approach in this problem
in general? A related consideration is the likelihood of observing zero
events at various N's and what we can conclude. Is a upper CI based on
the rule of 3's the best approach? (see Hanley JA. Lippman-Hand H. "If
Nothing Goes Wrong, Is Everything All Right?" Journal of the American
Medical Association. 1983; 249(3):1743-5)

I am very grateful for any instruction and guidance in this.

Drew Griffin Levy, Ph.D.
Senior Manager, Global Epidemiology
Aventis Pharmaceuticals
