From brandon.paris at kodak.com  Sun May 20 15:33:29 2001
From: brandon.paris at kodak.com
Date: Sun, 20 May 2001 11:33:29 -0400
Subject: Application of Statistical Distributions in Business
Message-ID: <85256A52.0055750E.00@knotes.kodak.com>

From: Brandon L. Paris

About a month ago I posted a question to the list about how various
probability distributions are used in market research.  I received a few
replies, most requesting that I post my final list back to S-News.  Here's
the (very) brief list I've compiled.  The original post is below.  Since
this is a personal endeavor and I have been unable to find a pre-existing
list, I'm making this a work in progress.  Hopefully over time I will be
able to augment and refine the list.

Brandon Paris

Logistic
Useful for projecting new product penetration (gained from a reply to my
post by Alan Gross.  He was kind to reference a paper by Fischer and Pry
published some 20-30 years ago (no date))

Beta
As I posted originally, has use in simulating market shares.

Lognormal
As posted originally, is the common distribution for sales data or income
data.

Extreme-Value
The result of sampling several times and taking the maximum (or minimum)
result.  This is the underlying distribution for discrete-choice methods.

t-, F-, and Chi-Square
Obvious uses as sampling distributions.

Cauchy
The ratio of two standard normal random variables.  Since ratios are
commonly used in business, this can be a problem since the moments are
undefined for this distribution.  Without any justification, I suspect that
the actual occurence of this distribution is rare, as standard normal
distributions occuring naturally are rare in business settings.  However, I
have learned from a colleague at work of two papers that address the
distribution of two normal random variables with arbitrary mean, variance
and correlation.  They are:

          Fieller, E.C. (1932), "The distribution of the index in a normal
bivariate population," Biometrika  24, 428-40.

          Hinckley, D. V. (1969), "On the Ratio of Two Correlated Normal
Random Variables," Biometrika, 56, 635-639.

I have not reviewed either yet but plan to do so.  These are probably more
relevant to business applications.

